Here's a step-by-step guide to creating a Chrome extension that saves the current page's content as an HTML file:

### 1. Create Project Files
Create a new folder with the following files:

#### `manifest.json`
```json
{
  "manifest_version": 3,
  "name": "Page Content Saver",
  "version": "1.0",
  "permissions": ["activeTab", "scripting", "downloads"],
  "action": {
    "default_popup": "popup.html",
    "default_icon": {
      "16": "icons/icon16.png",
      "48": "icons/icon48.png",
      "128": "icons/icon128.png"
    }
  },
  "icons": {
    "16": "icons/icon16.png",
    "48": "icons/icon48.png",
    "128": "icons/icon128.png"
  }
}
```

#### `popup.html`
```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
  <style>
    body { width: 200px; padding: 10px; }
    button { 
      width: 100%;
      padding: 10px;
      background: #4CAF50;
      color: white;
      border: none;
      cursor: pointer;
    }
  </style>
</head>
<body>
  <button id="saveButton">Save This Page</button>
  <script src="popup.js"></script>
</body>
</html>
```

#### `popup.js`
```javascript
document.getElementById('saveButton').addEventListener('click', () => {
  chrome.tabs.query({ active: true, currentWindow: true }, (tabs) => {
    const tab = tabs[0];

    if (!tab) {
      console.error('No active tab found');
      return;
    }

    chrome.scripting.executeScript({
      target: { tabId: tab.id },
      func: () => document.documentElement.outerHTML
    }, (results) => {
      if (chrome.runtime.lastError) {
        console.error(chrome.runtime.lastError.message);
        return;
      }

      if (!results || !results.length) {
        console.error('No results from content script');
        return;
      }

      const htmlContent = results[0].result;
      const filename = generateFilename(tab.title, tab.url);

      const dataUrl = `data:text/html;charset=utf-8,${encodeURIComponent(htmlContent)}`;

      chrome.downloads.download({
        url: dataUrl,
        filename: filename
      });
    });
  });
});

function generateFilename(title, url) {
  try {
    const urlObj = new URL(url);
    const hostname = urlObj.hostname;
    const cleanTitle = title.replace(/[^\w\s]/gi, '_').substring(0, 50);
    return `${cleanTitle}_${hostname}.html`.toLowerCase();
  } catch {
    return `page_content_${Date.now()}.html`;
  }
}
```

### 2. Add Icons
Create an `icons` folder and add these PNG files (you can use placeholders):
- icon16.png (16x16 pixels)
- icon48.png (48x48 pixels)
- icon128.png (128x128 pixels)

### 3. Install the Extension
1. Open Chrome and navigate to `chrome://extensions/`
2. Enable "Developer mode" (toggle in top-right corner)
3. Click "Load unpacked"
4. Select your project folder

### How to Use
1. Navigate to any webpage
2. Click the extension icon in the toolbar
3. Click "Save This Page"
4. The page content will be downloaded as an HTML file

### Features
- Saves complete page HTML
- Generates filenames using page title and domain
- Handles special characters in filenames
- Works on most websites
- Includes error handling

### Notes
- The extension only saves the HTML content (doesn't save linked resources)
- Some restricted pages (chrome:// pages) may not work
- Filenames are sanitized for compatibility
- Uses Chrome's Manifest V3 (latest standard)

You can customize the styling in `popup.html` and the filename generation logic in `popup.js` to match your preferences.

